22:52:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a great observation, and you’re absolutely right—72°F is 72°F no matter the season. The difference is that our bodies don’t sense temperature like a thermostat. We feel *heat loss*, not air temperature alone. In winter, even if the air is 72°F, you’re losing body heat faster because of four main factors: **1. Cold surfaces steal your radiant heat** Your body constantly radiates heat to colder surfaces around you (walls, windows, floors). In winter, exterior walls and windows can be far cooler than 72°F—sometimes 50–60°F. That temperature difference pulls more radiant heat from your skin, making you feel chilled even though the air is warm. It’s like standing next to a cold window: the air might be 72°, but your face feels the chill. This is why radiant heating (warm floors or panels) feels so cozy—it neutralizes that heat loss. **2. Dry air accelerates evaporative cooling** Cold outdoor air holds very little moisture. When it’s heated indoors to 72°F without humidification, the relative humidity can plummet to 20–30% (or lower). Dry air makes moisture evaporate faster from your skin, which cools you down—the same principle as sweat. In summer, higher humidity slows evaporation, so the same 72°F can feel muggy or even stuffy, but you’re losing less heat. So in winter, your personal microclimate is drier and your skin is constantly losing heat through evaporation. **3. Subtle drafts become more noticeable** Even well-sealed homes can have minor air movement near windows, doors, and vents. In winter, those tiny drafts of slightly cooler air feel more intrusive because they whisk away the thin warm layer of air your body creates around itself (convection). Your thermostat may read 72°, but a slight draft at ankle level can make the room feel drafty. **4. Your body’s seasonal acclimatization** Your internal “thermostat” adapts to seasonal patterns. After months of cold outdoor exposure, your body’s baseline for “neutral” shifts downward. When you come indoors to 72°F, it might feel chilly because your body is still in winter mode, expecting colder conditions. In summer, moving from heat into 72°F air conditioning feels refreshing because your body is acclimated to warmth. This psychological and physiological adaptation is real, though secondary to the physical heat-loss factors. So in practice, winter comfort at 72°F often requires addressing these invisible thieves: adding a humidifier, wearing an extra layer (even thin socks), or boosting the thermostat a couple degrees if you’re sitting near cold surfaces. Conversely, in summer, 72°F feels pleasant because you’re losing heat more slowly into warm outdoor surfaces and humid air.
