22:53:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair point—and you’ve done exactly the right thing by checking. If every room’s air thermometer reads a steady 72°F, the air really is uniform. But the reason you still feel colder in winter is that a basic thermometer only measures **air temperature**, not the other ways your body loses heat. The air can be the same everywhere, while you’re losing heat faster to unseen factors. When you say the house is “one uniform room temperature,” that’s true for the air. But your body is also exchanging heat with every surface around you—walls, windows, floor, ceiling—through radiation, and that doesn’t show up on a standard thermometer. Here’s why that matters: **1. Radiant temperature is separate from air temperature** In winter, exterior walls and windows are much colder than the indoor air (often 55–65°F even in a well-insulated house). They’re pulling heat out of your body via infrared radiation, even though the air touching them is 72°F. A thermometer hanging in the middle of the room only reads the air it’s in; it doesn’t “see” those cold surfaces. But your skin does—and that radiant heat loss makes you feel chilled. This is why on a sunny winter day, sitting near a cold window feels drafty, even if the air around you is toasty. The air is fine, but the window is stealing your heat. **2. Humidity still varies, even if the air temperature is uniform** Your thermometers don’t measure humidity. The same 72°F air in winter can have a drastically lower relative humidity than in summer—often below 30% indoors when it’s cold outside. That dry air accelerates evaporation from your skin (you’re not sweating, but there’s always insensible water loss) and cools you more. In summer, 72°F air with 60% humidity feels much warmer because evaporation is slower. The temperature reading is identical, but your body’s microclimate is different. **3. Micro-drafts don’t register on most thermometers** Even in a house with stable air temperature, tiny air currents near windows, baseboards, or doorways can exist. These drafts might be just 0.5–1°F cooler than room air, but moving air strips away the warm insulating layer your body creates. Your thermometer might be mounted on a wall away from these currents, or its response is too slow to catch a slight, transient dip. But your skin notices immediately. **4. What you’re measuring vs. what you’re feeling** A standard air thermometer gives you the dry-bulb temperature. Comfort scientists use “operative temperature,” which averages air temperature and the mean radiant temperature from surrounding surfaces. In winter, your operative temperature can be 68°F or lower even though the air is 72°F. That’s a 4°F drop in perceived comfort—no wonder it feels colder. So your experiment actually proves the point beautifully: the air *is* uniform, and yet you still feel the cold. That’s because comfort isn’t just about the air; it’s about how quickly your body loses heat to the room’s air *and* its surfaces. If you want to test this, you can try pointing an infrared thermometer at an exterior wall and an interior wall on the same day—you’ll often see a 10–15°F difference, even though the air around both is 72°F. That’s the hidden chill you’re picking up.
